WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.151
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:09.965
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:16.517
Unit eleven: Education Policy. Today you will emphasise key points with what-clauses.

4
00:00:16.817 --> 00:00:19.376
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.752
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:26.129
Public school funding.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:31.310
Public school funding means government money used to run state schools.

8
00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:34.659
Public school funding rose by five percent.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.274
Your turn. Say: Public school funding.

10
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.715
Public school funding.

11
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:44.881
Standardized testing.

12
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:50.100
Standardized testing means using the same exam to measure all students.

13
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.912
Standardized testing allows schools to be compared.

14
00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:57.912
Your turn. Say: Standardized testing.

15
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:02.360
Standardized testing.

16
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:04.420
Curriculum reform.

17
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:08.949
Curriculum reform means changes to what and how schools teach.

18
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.745
Curriculum reform added digital skills lessons.

19
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.188
Your turn. Say: Curriculum reform.

20
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:20.719
Curriculum reform.

21
00:01:21.483 --> 00:01:22.624
Teacher shortage.

22
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:28.043
Teacher shortage means a situation in which there are too few qualified teachers.

23
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.613
The teacher shortage forced schools to merge classes.

24
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:34.892
Your turn. Say: Teacher shortage.

25
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:38.851
Teacher shortage.

26
00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:40.989
School choice.

27
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:46.642
School choice means the freedom of families to pick which school a child attends.

28
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.505
School choice has supporters and critics.

29
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:52.839
Your turn. Say: School choice.

30
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.142
School choice.

31
00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:59.790
Higher education access.

32
00:02:00.783 --> 00:02:05.755
Higher education access means the chance for people to enter university or college.

33
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.197
Scholarships improve higher education access.

34
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:13.202
Your turn. Say: Higher education access.

35
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:17.973
Higher education access.

36
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:20.275
Education equity.

37
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:25.651
Education equity means fair opportunities in learning for every student.

38
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.538
Education equity means extra help where it is needed.

39
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.406
Your turn. Say: Education equity.

40
00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:38.324
Education equity.

41
00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:40.166
Dropout rate.

42
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.112
Dropout rate means the percentage of students who leave school early.

43
00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:48.622
The dropout rate fell after the new support programme.

44
00:02:49.217 --> 00:02:51.973
Your turn. Say: Dropout rate.

45
00:02:54.833 --> 00:02:56.113
Dropout rate.

46
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.261
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.117 --> 00:03:07.995
Visal, a minister says 'What schools need is more qualified teachers.' Why not simply 'Schools need more qualified teachers'?

48
00:03:09.083 --> 00:03:23.740
The pseudo-cleft, with what, puts the key information at the end for emphasis. What + subject + verb + is/was + focus: What worries parents is the dropout rate.

49
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.733
Can the focus be a clause?

50
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:40.940
Yes: What the reform did was to reduce standardized testing. Note that 'do' is used when the focus is an action: What the government must do is increase public school funding.

51
00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.748
Is it only for formal writing?

52
00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:56.279
No, it is strong in debates and speeches. All that students want is fair access to higher education, and the thing that matters is education equity.

53
00:03:57.433 --> 00:03:59.061
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:03.693
Grammar focus: Emphasis: What schools need is.

55
00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:05.214
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:05.917 --> 00:04:08.613
What schools need is more qualified teachers.

57
00:04:09.517 --> 00:04:12.364
What worries parents is the rising dropout rate.

58
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.503
What the government must do is increase public school funding.

59
00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:27.982
Pseudo-cleft: What + clause + is / was + focus. If the focus is an action, use do: What we must do is invest. It adds emphasis to the end of the sentence.

60
00:04:28.717 --> 00:04:32.658
Be careful! Don't say: What worries me it is the cost.

61
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.774
Say: What worries me is the cost.

62
00:04:37.074 --> 00:04:39.108
Do not add 'it' after the what-clause.

63
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.343
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:44.785
Rethinking Our Schools.

65
00:04:45.085 --> 00:04:49.801
Education policy affects every family, yet experts disagree about what works.

66
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:57.664
What many teachers report is a serious teacher shortage, and what critics question is the heavy use of standardized testing.

67
00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:05.612
What supporters of school choice want is freedom for parents, whereas others insist that public school funding must come first.

68
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:11.084
What curriculum reform must do is prepare students for work and citizenship.

69
00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:18.407
Ultimately, what every system should guarantee is higher education access for talented students from poor families.

70
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.931
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:27.410
What schools need blank more qualified teachers.

72
00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:30.585
A, are. B, is. C, have.

73
00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.994
The answer is B: is.

74
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.183
A lack of enough qualified teachers is a teacher blank.

75
00:05:40.483 --> 00:05:44.287
A, choice. B, reform. C, shortage.

76
00:05:47.833 --> 00:05:50.053
The answer is C: shortage.

77
00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:53.843
What the government must do blank increase funding.

78
00:05:54.143 --> 00:05:57.402
A, is to. B, does. C, has.

79
00:06:00.967 --> 00:06:02.769
The answer is A: is to.

80
00:06:03.917 --> 00:06:05.614
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:05.914 --> 00:06:12.540
A, What worries me it is the cost.. B, What worries me is the cost.. C, What worries me are the cost..

82
00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:19.295
The answer is B: What worries me is the cost..

83
00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:24.330
According to the reading, a system should guarantee higher education blank.

84
00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.168
A, debt. B, access. C, tests.

85
00:06:31.733 --> 00:06:34.255
The answer is B: access.

86
00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:38.514
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:43.892
Should standardized testing play a large role in evaluating students and schools?

88
00:06:52.683 --> 00:06:54.126
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:54.426 --> 00:07:22.773
Standardized testing can give a fair, comparable measure of what students have learned, and it can show which schools need help. However, relying on it too heavily may narrow the curriculum, increase stress and ignore creativity or teamwork. A balanced system would use tests as one source of evidence, together with teacher assessment, projects and each student's progress over time.

90
00:07:23.073 --> 00:07:24.783
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.683 --> 00:07:30.994
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and emphasis: what schools need is.

92
00:07:31.294 --> 00:07:36.756
Today's value is educational equity. Every learner deserves a fair chance to succeed.

93
00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:39.813
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:07:40.113 --> 00:07:42.673
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

